## Final decision (2026-07-25): additive-only, for good

Revisited at the end of week1, once `12_context` (the last Ruby/Python
port step) was done and the question became "how do we integrate MCP
once and for all" rather than "how do we integrate MCP for this specific
plan." Conclusion: **additive-only is the final architecture for
week1, not a placeholder pending a later harmonization pass.**
`Tools::Mud`/`tools/mud.py` remain the primary, single-agent MUD-tool
path in both languages; `mud_manager_mcp` stays exactly as built —
proven, tested, living alongside them.

**Why not go further and migrate `Tools::Mud` onto MCP clients**
(the "harmonization" path this plan always flagged as a separate,
deliberately-deferred step):

- The one thing MCP harmonization actually buys — a single MUD session
  multiple agent *processes* can share — has no live use case in week1.
  Every real session so far (`dummy`, `balthasar`, the `boukensha` test
  character) has been one agent process per character, never multiple
  processes sharing one login. Even the concurrency exploration in
  `week0_explore/explore_architecture/03_subagent_sdk` ran two
  *different* characters concurrently, each with its own independent
  session — not the same session shared.
- The migration would touch the single most heavily-used, most
  real-progress-bearing code path in the whole project (everything
  `dummy`'s actual leveling has run through), for a benefit that isn't
  exercised. It would also add a hard runtime dependency (the MCP
  server must already be running before any agent can play) and a
  second layer that can independently drop, on top of the MUD's own
  already-unpredictable disconnects.
- Sequencing made this concrete rather than hypothetical: this decision
  was made right before a live leveling playtest of the Python
  TUI/REPL build. Rewriting the MUD-tool path immediately beforehand
  would have put that playtest's success at direct risk for an
  architectural nicety with no exercised payoff.
- This tracks the user's own week1 retro note (`docs/journal/1_week1.md`)
  that the earlier mid-flight MCP-driven refactor discussion "introduced
  enormous complexity for comparatively little value," and that the
  better throughline would have been incremental, need-driven MCP
  adoption rather than a wholesale architecture change.

**What would change this decision**: a real, concrete need for multiple
agent processes (same or different language) to share one MUD login
simultaneously — e.g. a week2 revisit of multi-agent concurrency that
specifically wants shared-session semantics rather than independent
characters. Until then, this file describes a finished, standalone
exploration, not a pending migration.
